Time Complexity: tells us about growth in runtime of that algorithm wrt its input 
Space complexity: tells us about the growth in ram memory since all algo reside in ram, wrt input 

Time complexity is measured with regards to numbers of operations your code performs. These number of operations is converted to big Oh (O)
For space complexity calculation, data and variables are taken in account 

Big Oh notation: when an algo has a T.C of O(N), it means its run time is proportional to N: which means its linear 
O(1): constant time complexity, meaning no matter what the input is, its T.C remains same 
O(N²): quadratic time complexity, for every input, T.C gets squared 
O(logN): 
best one is O(1) -> O(logN) -> O(N) -> O(N²)

example: for input of 100 million operations 
1) O(1): constant, fixed T.C (fixed number of operations for any input)
2) O(logN) = O(log₂(100 mil)) = 27 operations 
3) O(N) = 100 mil operations 
4) O(N²) = (100 mil operations)²
